    Let $G$ be a definable group of finite dimension and $\{H_i\}_{i\in I}$ a family of uniformly definable subgroups. Then, there exists $n,d<\omega$ such that there is no $J=\{j_1,...,j_n\}\subseteq I$ of cardinality $n$ such that $|\bigcap_{i=1}^kH_{j_i}:\bigcap_{i=1}^{k+1} H_{j_i}|$ has index greater than $d$ for any $k\leq n-1$.
