\subsection*{Notation}
Most of the notation in the paper is standard, for example, $\phi(n)$ is Euler's totient function, for a finite set $S$, $\#S$ is the number of elements of $S$, $\lfloor x\rfloor$ is the floor of $x$, for a pair of integers $a,b$, $(a,b) = \gcd(a,b)$. We also use Landau's $O$ notation, i.e., $f(x) = O(g(x))$ if there exists a positive constant $C$ such that $|f(x)| \le C|g(x)|$ for large enough $x$. A notation we use that is less well-known is $O^*$. We say that $f(x) = O^*(g(x))$ if for all $x\ge x_0$, $|f(x)| \le |g(x)|$.

